WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:15.609
There are three particular things I want to
draw the Board's attention to in the paper.

00:00:15.609 --> 00:00:23.070
The first one is to give the Board assurance
that NHS England, in its operational oversight

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.840
of the system, is ready to deal with the inevitable
pressures that winter will bring.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:37.740
The NHS always puts in place very significant
plans for winter and changes the way that

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:43.010
it operationally manages the system during
that time, because there is inevitably greater

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:49.690
general demand, but there are also more spikes
of demand and much more fluctuation.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.910
As commissioners, we have responsibility to
secure services for our patients; with our

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.640
oversight role of CCGs, as well, it means
that we have to ensure that comprehensive

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.200
services for patients are available at all
times.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.790
Much of what we do is about routine flow,
but there are times when we need to be able

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:13.220
to ensure that the system can flex and change
and respond to demand.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:19.251
That might be a sudden onset in change in
demand nationally, but it may well be different

00:01:19.251 --> 00:01:21.810
things happening in different parts of the
country.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:28.729
At the very extreme of this, we undertake
emergency planning and we would change the

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:37.100
way in which we operated very significantly
if there was a major emergency; but more commonly

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.940
for us, fortunately, is the routine dealing
with winter.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:48.509
Urgent care
We have in place urgent care teams, networks

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:53.850
and boards across England who have been looking
at their own local plans and this paper starts

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:59.510
to talk about the things that we will start
to do routinely nationally, regionally and

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:06.860
at area, CCG and Health &amp; Wellbeing Board
level as the winter moves on.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:13.980
We know that the need to keep the integrity
of the Service at all times will be more difficult

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.579
but is equally important.

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:24.090
It is particularly important in instances
where, perhaps, one hospital, because it may

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:29.160
have an outbreak of a virus causing diarrhoea,
has to close beds; or it may well be that

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:35.340
there is a particular virus or flu illness
in that area, where one hospital feels that

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.959
it just doesn't have the capacity to continue
to take emergency admissions and therefore

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:47.489
we have to work with other local providers
to ensure that those emergencies can be received.

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:51.909
Rare services
It is often important for rare services.

00:02:51.909 --> 00:02:56.190
It is not only important for routine services;
it is about the rare things we offer, such

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.169
as paediatric intensive care beds.

00:02:59.169 --> 00:03:04.239
There are times when one particular area needs
more paediatric intensive care beds than it

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:09.439
has and we need to be able to ensure that
children can be safely treated somewhere else,

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:11.159
should that need arise.

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:16.791
I think one of the very best examples where
the NHS did a great job, particularly when

00:03:16.791 --> 00:03:20.930
we had the last flu, was ECMO: extracorporeal
oxygenation.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:27.239
That is a situation whereby, when a patient
cannot breathe because they are so ill with

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:32.469
flu and their lungs are not working properly,
there is a way of keeping them alive by them

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:35.799
getting oxygen outside their body.

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:42.559
Clearly, it would not be possible for the
NHS to have vast numbers of these very specialist

00:03:42.559 --> 00:03:49.379
beds and the specialists who can use them
but actually, what we were able to do when

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:53.799
we had the last flu pandemic was, certainly,
to move people around as they needed those

00:03:53.799 --> 00:04:00.099
very precious beds and also to increase the
quantities by being able to move some of the

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:04.769
intensive and high dependency beds that we
had, to be able to undertake that sort of

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:05.769
role.

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:08.669
Concluding remarks
This paper takes the Board through everything

00:04:08.669 --> 00:04:09.669
that we have in place.

00:04:09.669 --> 00:04:14.999
This is now very much a joint effort, working
very closely with our partners in TDA and

00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:20.299
Monitor and with our local government partners.

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:23.280
The work is well underway to get us ready
for the winter.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.360
Government money
Targeting

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:30.370
If I can then move on from that, one of the
particular things that has happened is that

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:38.720
the Government has given the NHS an extra
£250 million for this year and for next year

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.229
specifically to deal with the pressures that
come in our A&amp;E departments.

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:50.860
Again, we all know, when it gets very busy,
the pressure point of the NHS is the accident

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.280
and emergency department.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.990
In their concern to ensure that the A&amp;E targets
are met and that patients are not waiting

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:05.330
in the A&amp;E department unnecessarily for a
long time -- usually to be admitted and therefore

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:13.340
sick patients -- the £250 million was to
be targeted at the Trusts most likely or seen

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:18.030
to be struggling most with meeting the A&amp;E
targets.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:24.590
The Trusts were identified by Monitor and
TDA, who oversee the organisations and they

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:31.210
identified 53 Trusts as being most at risk
of not being able to meet the waiting times

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.789
for patients as we go through the winter.

00:05:34.789 --> 00:05:40.449
Those are the areas that we have targeted
the money at, but not just within the Trusts

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:46.009
themselves because often, as we all know,
the reasons why A&amp;E departments are struggling

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:51.440
is because too many patients are attending
who could perhaps have been better dealt with

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:52.610
at home.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:56.830
The A&amp;E departments are not able to admit
patients because the wards are full, whereas

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:01.139
if there had been better services in the community,
then those patients would have been able to

00:06:01.139 --> 00:06:02.139
go home sooner.

00:06:02.139 --> 00:06:05.479
It is better for them and it is better for
the other patients who are waiting for the

00:06:05.479 --> 00:06:06.949
beds.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:11.960
Allocation breakdown
Where this £250 million is going has now

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.319
been determined.

00:06:13.319 --> 00:06:19.310
£15 million of that is going to support 111,
which will obviously be a critical service

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:24.039
in the winter; and we hope with that £15
million to ensure that we can deal with an

00:06:24.039 --> 00:06:27.169
extra 20,000 calls.

00:06:27.169 --> 00:06:32.610
It should include us having about 200 more
call handlers and an extra 60 clinicians through

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.520
the winter for 111.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:41.539
The other £235 million, as I say, has been
distributed across the 53 Trusts and a list

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:43.610
of those Trusts is available.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:50.219
In general, about £62 million of the £235
million is going for extra capacity in hospitals:

00:06:50.219 --> 00:06:54.930
additional staff, additional ways of working
and additional equipment.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:02.289
About another £60 million -- £57 million
-- is on community services, which are particularly

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:05.449
end of life care and support for hospices.

00:07:05.449 --> 00:07:11.139
Certainly, within hospitals, it is a full
range of things: it is not just about additional

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:20.860
A&amp;E consultants -- because there is a shortage
of A&amp;E doctors and there are only so many

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.220
to go round -- but it is about things like
making sure that where patients with mental

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:29.340
health issues are admitted to hospital, the
right resource is available to them quickly

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.820
to have them dealt with, if necessary, in
the A&amp;E department.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:39.629
About £50 million is for preventative care,
to make sure that we have fewer episodes and

00:07:39.629 --> 00:07:41.770
that people are kept well.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:46.490
About £25 million will go specifically into
primary care initiatives; and about £16 million

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:47.490
into social care.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.139
Change of name to 'Urgent Care Working Groups'
That was the second thing that I wanted to

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:52.270
draw out of here.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:55.689
The third one is a change of name.

00:07:55.689 --> 00:08:01.939
If you remember, in May we asked our area
teams to convene what we then called 'Urgent

00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:07.249
Care Boards' across England, to make sure
that managers and clinicians locally came

00:08:07.249 --> 00:08:11.039
together to determine how best they could
deal with winter.

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:15.599
The Health Select Committee rightly pointed
out that we had caused some confusion in the

00:08:15.599 --> 00:08:20.319
system, I think by using the name 'Board'
and by not being absolutely clear about the

00:08:20.319 --> 00:08:25.790
nature of the groups and that there was the
potential, therefore, to undermine the authority

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:31.009
of the Health and Wellbeing Boards; that the
name 'Board' gave a suggestion that they would

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:32.870
have more authority than they did.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:38.310
They are vitally important groups of clinicians
and managers, coming together to work out

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:43.089
how they can make a difference for patients
and what they need to do; and they bring their

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:47.240
own authority, build consensus and decide
how to coordinate.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:53.790
I think it was good advice and what I would
like for the Board to agree is that we change

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:59.420
the name to 'Urgent Care Working Group' and
that I will write out to the system to clarify

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.560
the role and relationship with the Health
and Wellbeing Board.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:07.000
I will not, however, be very prescriptive
about the nature of these groups, because

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:12.270
we feel it is absolutely vital that people
determine locally who should be on these groups,

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:15.040
to be the best that they can be for them,
locally.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.740
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Barbara.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:21.529
Are there any comments or questions from Members
of the Board?

00:09:21.529 --> 00:09:23.329
No, thank you.

00:09:23.329 --> 00:09:24.529
Yes, Moira?

00:09:24.529 --> 00:09:27.974
Moira Gibb: Thank you for that, Barbara, it
was very helpful.

00:09:27.974 --> 00:09:32.390
It did feel to me reading it, however, that
it was how things would have been last year

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.050
and the year before and the year before but
that actually, we have just gone through an

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:38.839
amazing change within the NHS in terms of
the organisations.

00:09:38.839 --> 00:09:45.940
The first question is: is it just as straightforward
to coordinate in this way and to do this coordination?

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:51.720
Will the new system, however, actually allow
us to do more of that prevention work, as

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:57.360
opposed to operationally managing the system?

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.510
Can I add to that a question that was put
to me when I was out on a visit?

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:10.509
That is: are we nationally going to be sending
some messages out to the public about the

00:10:10.509 --> 00:10:12.779
use of A&amp;E?

00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:16.870
We have had lots of discussion about social
media and about information coming to us,

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:22.610
but are we going to use social media in this
environment, as well, to send messages to

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:26.320
people about not coming inappropriately to
A&amp;E?

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:29.170
Barbara Hakin: Thank you, Moira.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:34.589
I think there are two different issues, which
we should not conflate.

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:40.690
It is absolutely right and we are trying really
hard in NHS England to make sure that locally,

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:47.290
people can transform the way they deliver
services, have as much freedom as possible

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:53.800
to identify what works for them and to make
the whole of the health and social care system

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.040
better.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:03.600
As part of that, we are trying not to ask
them for a lot of information that is just

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:08.680
part of a top down initiative in terms of
the way they generally want to develop and

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.110
support their health services.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:15.250
I think that is different from the operational
management of the NHS, whereby it is absolutely

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:20.060
essential for patients across England that
we are in receipt of this information which

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:25.449
is collected and collated and can be acted
on locally, regionally or nationally, depending

00:11:25.449 --> 00:11:29.519
on the nature of the service, so that we can
give assurance to the public that our emergency

00:11:29.519 --> 00:11:31.329
services, in particular, will be available
at all times.

00:11:31.329 --> 00:11:37.560
As to your second question about messaging,
this will be jointly between us and Public

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:42.640
Health England because, of course, a very
significant amount of the messages that help

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:50.720
us over the winter are public health messages,
such as, 'Catch it, Bin it, Kill it' initiative

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.160
that we had, I think, the year before last.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:57.120
We are therefore working very closely with
the Department of Health and Public Health

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:05.399
England, who will be doing a major initiative
for winter and as part of that, they have

00:12:05.399 --> 00:12:09.329
announced additional vaccinations and immunisations
for children.

00:12:09.329 --> 00:12:16.310
They are doing a major campaign and Tim's
team are working with them on the very specific

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:20.759
things that we would want to add, which are
more operational, about when to use A&amp;E, the

00:12:20.759 --> 00:12:23.139
use of 111 and those sorts of things.

00:12:23.139 --> 00:12:28.130
David Nicholson: May I say something about
the £250 million?

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:30.850
I think it is important to know.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:35.130
Every year, I think, for the last five years,
we have put out extra money for winter pressures.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:39.120
The difference this time is that we are doing
it earlier, which I think is a good thing.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:43.010
Very often it has been the week, or two or
three weeks before Christmas before we have

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:47.931
been able to do it, but this time to give
people the opportunity to plan properly is,

00:12:47.931 --> 00:12:51.470
I think, an important different place.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:55.880
The other thing, however, that we are differently
is that we are targeting the money on particular

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.569
organisations or healthcare systems.

00:12:58.569 --> 00:13:05.730
I think it is worth pointing out that the
whole thing about the four hour wait in A&amp;E

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:09.779
is that it is an indicator of pressure in
the system as a whole.

00:13:09.779 --> 00:13:14.750
As Barbara says, you do not, then, invest
the £250 million in A&amp;E; you have to invest

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:18.300
it in the system as a whole to make it happen.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:22.260
What is really important for those people
who have the money is to realise that part

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:26.900
of the reason for giving them the money is
for them to come to sustainable solutions

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.209
for the way in which their systems operates.

00:13:29.209 --> 00:13:32.620
This is absolutely not sticking plaster, in
that sense.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.550
It is very important to know that.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:40.000
One point, as well, is that there is not another
set of money coming out to the Service later.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:41.050
This is it.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:45.320
This is the total amount of extra money which
is going out into the system for winter.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:49.899
I think it is important to underscore that
as we go forward.

00:13:49.899 --> 00:13:52.470
This winter is going to be a big challenge
for the NHS.

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:56.459
There is pressure on the system.

00:13:56.459 --> 00:13:58.370
I think people are managing remarkably well.

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:03.980
I think NHS staff will be very committed to
making all of this work.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:09.280
I think the pinch points will not just be
in A&amp;E but in paediatric intensive care and

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:12.970
intensive care units generally and that is
why the capacity planning is so important,

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:16.600
to ensure we have the right numbers is all
of these places.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:21.620
That whole system management, not the management
of one particular bit of the system -- i.e.

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:25.339
A&amp;E departments -- will make the difference
between success and failure.

00:14:25.339 --> 00:14:27.540
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, David.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:31.181
May we thank Barbara for the report and for
the assurance of readiness for winter?

00:14:31.181 --> 00:14:32.181
I would like to move on now, please, to item
eight on the agenda, which is a series of

00:14:32.181 --> 00:14:33.181
reports from Board Committees.

00:14:33.181 --> 00:14:34.181
Board Committees do a lot of the work of the
Board and many of them have executive responsibility,

00:14:34.181 --> 00:14:35.181
subject always to reporting back to the Board
itself.

00:14:35.181 --> 00:14:35.197
Victor, I wonder if we might kick off with
you with the CCG authorisation and assurance.

